// A method on a generic type. Marked xfail: no method on a generic struct can
// be called, whatever its signature.
//
// Instantiation clones the struct and its methods together, but the clone's
// 'self' parameter keeps the type of the generic rather than the instance, so
// every method of every instance is rejected with
//
//   self parameter for a method must match, or be a reference to, its type
//
// reported at the method's declaration and again at the instantiation site,
// once per instantiation. The call then finds no candidate. This file uses the
// simplest method there is -- no type parameter in its signature, a constant
// body -- so that when it starts passing, what was fixed is unambiguous.
//
// This is why generic-success declares only field-holding generic types, and
// why 'boxUp' is a free generic function rather than a constructor method.
//
// When this scenario starts passing, drop the xfail and fold it into
// generic-success as a printed fact.

struct Box[T] {
  v T

  fn get(self) i64 {
    7i64
  }
}

fn useIt() i64 {
  imm b = Box[i64][v: 1i64]
  b.get()
}
